Docker volume
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
a. Named volume: A named volume is storage managed by Docker that is stored separately from the container and can persist even after the container is deleted. Use a named volume when you want Docker to manage persistent data.
Commands:
	1. docker volume create <volume name>: create a name volume 
	2. docker volume ls : list all volume which are defined
	3. docker volume inspect <volume name> : Show the detailed information
	4. docker run --name <conatiner name> -v <volume name>:/app/data <image name>
	5. docker volume rm <volume name> : it delete the volume. before we need to delete container
b. Bind volume: A bind mount maps a specific folder or file from the host machine directly to a folder or file inside the container.Use a bind mount when you want the container to directly access files on your computer.
b. tmpfs mount: A tmpfs mount stores temporary data in the host's memory (RAM) instead of persistent disk storage.
